225 227 1734 (          The Transient Program Area, TPA, is the region of memory where transient pro-) 1734 SB
225 286 1172 (        grams execute.  The CCP also executes in this region.) 1172 SB
225 405 1765 (          The Console Command Processor, CCP, is not an operating system module, but is) 1765 SB
225 464 1743 (        a system program that presents a human-oriented interface to CP/M 3 for the user.) 1743 SB
225 582 1664 (          The Page Zero region is not an operating system module either, but functions) 1664 SB
225 641 1782 (        primarily as an interface to the BDOS module from the CCP and transient programs.) 1782 SB
225 700 944 (        It also contains critical system parameters.) 944 SB
225 820 91 (       ) 91 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
316 819 1206 ( 1.3 System Component Interaction and Communication) 1206 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 938 1704 (          This section describes interaction and communication between the modules and) 1704 SB
225 997 1749 (        regions defined in Section 1.2. The most significant channels of communication are) 1749 SB
225 1056 1755 (        between the BDOS and the BIOS, transient programs and the BDOS, and transient) 1755 SB
225 1116 527 (        programs and RSXS.) 527 SB
225 1234 1663 (          The division of responsibility between the different modules and the way they) 1663 SB
225 1293 1750 (        communicate with one another provide three important benefits.  First, because the) 1750 SB
225 1352 1700 (        operating system is divided into two modules-one that is configured for different) 1700 SB
225 1411 1734 (         ardware environments, and one that remains constant on every computer-CP/M 3) 1734 SB
225 1471 1769 (        software is hardware independent; you can port your programs unchanged to differ-) 1769 SB
225 1530 1765 (        ent hardware configurations.  Second, because all communication between transient) 1765 SB
225 1589 1804 (        programs and the BDOS is channeled through Page Zero, CP/M 3 transient programs) 1804 SB
225 1648 1729 (        execute, if sufficient memory is available, independent of configured memory size.) 1729 SB
225 1707 1769 (        Third, the CP/M 3 RSX facility can customize the services of CP/M 3 on a selective) 1769 SB
225 1766 213 (        basis.) 213 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 1885 678 (        1.3.1 The BDOS and BIOS) 678 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 2003 1772 (          CP/M 3 achieves hardware independence through the interface between the BDOS) 1772 SB
225 2062 1777 (        and the BIOS modules of the operating system.  This interface consists of a series of) 1777 SB
225 2121 1776 (        entry points in the BIOS that the BDOS calls to perform hardware-dependent primi-) 1776 SB
225 2181 1829 (        tive functions such as peripheral device VO.  For example, the BDOS calls the CONIN) 1829 SB
225 2240 1387 (        entry point of the BIOS to read the next console input character.) 1387 SB
225 2358 1764 (          A system implementor can customize the BIOS to match a specific hardware envi-) 1764 SB
225 2417 1807 (        ronment.  However, even when the BIOS primitives are customized to match the host) 1807 SB
225 2476 1691 (        computer's hardware environment, the BIOS entry points and the BDOS remain) 1691 SB
225 2536 1830 (        constant.  Therefore, the BDOS and the BIOS modules work together to give the CCP) 1830 SB
225 2595 1710 (        and other transient programs hardware-independent access to CP/M 3's facilities.) 1710 SB
1166 2831 67 (1-7) 67 SB
225 92 1946 ( 1.2 System Components                                                                  CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide ) 1946 SB
