225 1521 1449 (                 Figure 1-6.  System Modules and Regions in Logical Memory) 1449 SB
225 1639 1685 (        First note that all memory regions in CP/M 3 are page-aligned.  This means that) 1685 SB
225 1699 1693 (      regions and operating system modules must begin on a page boundary.  A page is) 1693 SB
225 1758 1762 (      defined as 256 bytes, so a page boundary always begins at an address where the low-) 1762 SB
225 1817 439 (      order byte is zero.) 439 SB
225 1935 1606 (        The term High Memory in Figure 1-6 denotes the high address of a CP/M 3) 1606 SB
225 1994 1771 (      system.  This address may fall below the actual top of memory address if space above) 1771 SB
225 2054 1693 (      the operating system has been allocated for directory hashing or data buffering by) 1693 SB
225 2113 1720 (      GENCPM.  The maximum top of memory address for both banked and nonbanked) 1720 SB
225 2172 656 (      systems is 64K-1 \(OFFFFH\).) 656 SB
225 2290 1811 (        T'he labels BIOS-base, BDOS_base, and LOADER_base represent the base addresses) 1811 SB
225 2349 1796 (      of the operating system regions.  These addresses always fall on page boundaries.  The) 1796 SB
225 2409 1680 (      size of the BIOS region is not fixed, but is determined by the requirements of the) 1680 SB
225 2468 525 (      host computer system.) 525 SB
