895 1148 609 (Figure 1-7.  Disk Organization) 609 SB
225 1266 1684 (     In Figure 1-7, the first N tracks are the system tracks.  System tracks are required) 1684 SB
225 1325 1575 (   only on the disk used by CP/M 3 during system cold start or warm start.  The) 1575 SB
225 1385 1721 (   contents of this region are described in Section 1.6.1. All normal CP/M 3 disk access) 1721 SB
225 1444 1317 (   is directed to the data tracks which CP/M 3 uses for file storage.) 1317 SB
225 1562 1707 (     The data tracks are divided into two regions: a directory area and a data area.  The) 1707 SB
225 1621 1646 (   directory area defines the files that exist on the drive and identifies the data space) 1646 SB
225 1680 1739 (   that belongs to each file.  The data area contains the file data defined by the directory.) 1739 SB
225 1740 1635 (   If the drive has adequate storage, a CP/M 3 file can be as large as 32 megabytes.) 1635 SB
225 1858 1752 (     The directory area is subdivided into sixteen logically independent directories.  These) 1752 SB
225 1917 1667 (   directories are identified by user numbers 0 through 15.  During system operation,) 1667 SB
225 1976 1643 (   CP/M 3 runs with the user number set to a single value.  The user number can be) 1643 SB
225 2035 1684 (   changed at the console with the USER command.  A transient program can change) 1684 SB
225 2095 930 (   the user number by calling a BDOS function.) 930 SB
225 2213 1670 (     The user number specifies the currently active directories for all the drives on the) 1670 SB
225 2272 1692 (   svstem.  For example, a PIP command to copy a file from one disk to another gives) 1692 SB
225 2331 1715 (   t@e destination file the same user number as the source file unless the PIP command) 1715 SB

