225 227 1665 (          The directory identifies each file with an eight-character filename and a three-) 1665 SB
225 286 1780 (        character filetype.  Together, these fields must be unique for each file.  Files with the) 1780 SB
225 345 1747 (        same filename and filetype can reside in different user directories on the same drive) 1747 SB
225 405 1693 (        without conflict.  Under the banked version of CP/M 3, a file can be assigned an) 1693 SB
225 464 1493 (        eight-character password to protect the file from unauthorized access.) 1493 SB
225 582 1726 (          All BDOS functions that involve file operations specify the requested file by file-) 1726 SB
225 641 1733 (        name and filetype.  Multiple files can be specified by a technique called ambiguous) 1733 SB
225 700 1713 (        reference, which uses question marks and asterisks as wildcard characters to give) 1713 SB
225 760 1653 (        CP/M 3 a pattern to match as it searches the directory.  A question mark in an) 1653 SB
225 819 1791 (        ambiguous reference matches any value in the same position in the directory filename) 1791 SB
225 878 1784 (        or filetype field.  An asterisk fills the remainder of the filename or filetype field of the) 1784 SB
225 937 1749 (        ambiguous reference with question marks.  Thus, a filename and filetype field of all) 1749 SB

