SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 608 (   1.6.1 Cold Start Operation) 608 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 1657 (      The cold start procedure is typically executed immediately after the computer is) 1657 SB
225 405 1636 (   turned on.  The cold start brings CP/M 3 into memory and gives it control of the) 1636 SB
225 464 1215 (   computer's resources.  Cold start is a four-stage procedure.) 1215 SB
225 582 1623 (      In the first stage, a hardware feature, or ROM-based software associated with) 1623 SB
225 641 1722 (   system reset, loads a small program, called the Cold Boot Loader, into memory from) 1722 SB
225 700 1747 (   the system tracks of drive A \(see figure 1-6\).  The Cold Boot Loader is usually 128 or) 1747 SB
225 760 343 (   256 bytes long.) 343 SB
225 878 1742 (      The Cold Boot Loader performs the second stage of the cold start process.  It loads) 1742 SB
225 937 1688 (   the CP/M 3 loader program, CPMLDR, into memory from the system tracks of the) 1688 SB
225 996 1662 (   system disk and passes control to it.  During this stage, the Cold Boot Loader can) 1662 SB
225 1055 1530 (   also perform other tasks, such as initializing hardware dependent I/O ports.) 1530 SB
225 1174 1628 (      CPMLDR performs the third stage in the cold start process.  First, it reads the) 1628 SB
225 1233 1729 (   CPM3.SYS file from the data area of the disk.  The CPM3.SYS file, which is created) 1729 SB
225 1292 1669 (   by the CP/M 3 system generation utility GENCPM, contains the BDOS and BIOS) 1669 SB
225 1351 1695 (   system components and information indicating where these modules are to reside in) 1695 SB
225 1410 1690 (   memory.  Once CPMLDR has loaded the BDOS and BIOS into memory, it sends a) 1690 SB
225 1470 1754 (   sign-on message to the console and passes control to the BIOS Cold Boot entry point.) 1754 SB
225 1529 1698 (   If specified as a GENCPM option, CPMLDR can also display a memory map of the) 1698 SB
225 1588 357 (   CP/M 3 system.) 357 SB
225 1706 1695 (      CPMLDR is a small, self-contained version of CP/M 3 that supports only console) 1695 SB
225 1765 1688 (   output and sequential file input.  Consistent with CP/M 3's organization, it contains) 1688 SB
225 1825 1863 (   two modules, an invariant CPMLDR-BDOS, and a variant CPMLDR              -BIOS that is) 1863 SB
225 1884 1714 (   adapted to match the host microcomputer hardware environment.  Cold start initiali-) 1714 SB
225 1943 1759 (   zation of VO ports and similar functions can also be performed in the CPMLDR-BIOS) 1759 SB
225 2002 889 (   module during the third stage of cold start.) 889 SB
225 2120 1740 (      In the banked version of CP/M 3, these first three stages of the cold boot procedure) 1740 SB
225 2180 1653 (   are performed with Bank 0 in context.  The BIOS Cold Start function switches in) 1653 SB
225 2239 832 (   Bank 1 before proceeding to stage four.) 832 SB
1154 2830 92 (1-14) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
EJ RS
%%PageTrailer
SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 1713 (           The fourth and final stage in the cold start procedure is performed by the BIOS) 1713 SB
225 286 1621 (        Cold Start function, Function 0. The entry point to this function is located at) 1621 SB
225 345 1701 (        BIOS -base as described in Section 1.4. The BIOS Cold Start function begins by) 1701 SB
225 405 1695 (        performing any remaining hardware initialization, and initializing Page Zero.  To) 1695 SB
225 464 1751 (        initialize Page Zero, the BIOS Cold Start function places a jump to BIOS base + 3,) 1751 SB
225 523 1834 (        the BIOS Warm Start entry point, at location OOOOH, and a jump to BDOS-base + 6,) 1834 SB
225 582 1161 (        the BDOS entry point, at location 0005H in memory.) 1161 SB
225 700 1770 (           The BIOS Cold Start function completes the fourth stage by loading the CCP into) 1770 SB
225 760 1748 (        the TPA region of memory and passing control to it.  The CCP can be loaded from) 1748 SB
225 819 1765 (        one of two locations.  If there is sufficient space in the system tracks for the CCP, it) 1765 SB
225 878 1731 (        is usually loaded from there.  If there is not enough space in the system tracks, the) 1731 SB
225 937 1476 (        BIOS Cold Start function can read the CCP from the file CCP.COM.) 1476 SB
225 1055 1682 (           On some banked systems, the CCP is also copied to an alternate bank, so that) 1682 SB
225 1115 1796 (        warm start operations can copy the CCP into the TPA from memory.  This speeds up) 1796 SB
225 1174 1690 (        the system warm start operation, and makes it possible to warm start the system) 1690 SB
225 1233 884 (        without having to access a system disk.) 884 SB
225 1351 1760 (           When the CCP gains control, it displays a prompt that references the default disk.) 1760 SB
225 1410 1776 (        If a PROFILE.SUB submit file is present on the default drive, the CCP executes this) 1776 SB
225 1470 1163 (        submit file before prompting the user for a command.) 1163 SB
225 1588 1732 (           At this point, the cold start procedure is complete.  Note that the user number is) 1732 SB
225 1647 1786 (        set to zero when CP/M 3 is cold started.  However, the PROFILE submit file can set) 1786 SB
225 1706 1126 (        the user number to another value if this is desirable.) 1126 SB
225 1825 1788 (           The cold start procedure is designed so that the system tracks need to be initialized) 1788 SB
225 1884 1805 (        only once.  This is accomplished because the system track routines are independent of) 1805 SB
225 1943 1710 (        the configured memory size of the CP/M 3 system.  The Cold Boot Loader loads) 1710 SB
225 2002 1693 (        CPMLDR into a constant location in memory.  This location is chosen when the) 1693 SB
225 2061 1806 (        system is configured.  However, CPMLDR locates the BDOS and BIOS system com-) 1806 SB
225 2120 1784 (        ponents in memory as specified by the CPM3.SYS file.  The CCP always executes at) 1784 SB
225 2180 1762 (        location 100H in the TPA.  Thus, CP/M 3 allows the user to generate a new system) 1762 SB
225 2239 1720 (        with GENCPM, and then run it without having to update the system tracks of the) 1720 SB
225 2298 346 (        system disk.) 346 SB
1154 2830 92 (1-15) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
