225 227 1680 (         All command keywords that do not identify built-in commands identify either a) 1680 SB
225 286 1778 (         transient program file or a submit file.  If the CCP identifies a command keyword as) 1778 SB
225 345 1776 (         a transient program, the transient program file is loaded into the TPA from disk and) 1776 SB
225 405 1786 (         executed.  If it recognizes a submit file, the CCP reconstructs the command line into) 1786 SB
225 464 501 (         the following form:) 501 SB
225 582 956 (             SUBMIT <command> <command tail>) 956 SB
225 700 1769 (         and attempts to load and execute the SUBMIT utility.  Thus, the original command) 1769 SB
225 760 1778 (         fie'ld becomes the first command tail field of the SUBMIT command.  Section 1.6.5) 1778 SB
225 819 1828 (         describes the execution of CP/M 3's SUBMIT utility.  The procedure the CCP follows) 1828 SB
225 878 1706 (         o parse a standard command line and execute built-in and transient commands is) 1706 SB
225 937 532 (         described as follows:) 532 SB
225 996 117 (         ) 117 SB
225 1055 1510 (             1. The CCP parses the command line to pick up the command field.) 1510 SB
225 1174 1731 (             2. If the command field is not preceded by a drive specification, or followed by) 1731 SB
225 1233 1723 (                a filetype or password field, the CCP checks to see if the command is a CCP) 1723 SB
225 1292 1674 (                built-in function.  If the command is a built-in command, and the CCP can) 1674 SB
225 1351 1636 (                support the options specified in the command tail, the CCP executes the) 1636 SB
225 1410 1204 (                command.  Otherwise, the CCP goes on to step 3.) 1204 SB
225 1529 1734 (             3. At this point the CCP assumes the command field references a command file) 1734 SB
225 1588 1711 (                or submit file on disk.  If the optional filetype field is omitted from the com-) 1711 SB
225 1647 1692 (                mand, the CCP usually assumes the command field references a file of type) 1692 SB
225 1706 1710 (                COM.  For example, if the command field is PIP, the CCP attempts to open) 1710 SB
225 1765 568 (                the file PIP.COM.) 568 SB
225 1884 1675 (                Optionally, the CP/M 3 utility SETDEF can specify that a filetype of SUB) 1675 SB
225 1943 1677 (                also be considered when the command filetype field is omitted.  When this) 1677 SB
225 2002 1728 (                automatic submit option is in effect, the CCP attempts to open the command) 1728 SB
225 2061 1696 (                with a filetype of COM.  If the COM file cannot be found, the CCP repeats) 1696 SB
225 2120 1685 (                the open op  eration with a filetype of SUB. As an alternative, the order of) 1685 SB
225 2180 1657 (                open operations can be reversed so that the CCP attempts to open with a) 1657 SB
225 2239 1713 (                filetype of SUB first.  In either case, the file that is found on disk first deter-) 1713 SB
225 2298 1605 (                mines the filetype field that is ultimately associated with the command.) 1605 SB
225 2357 1688 (                If the filetype field is present in the command, it must equal COM, SUB or) 1688 SB
225 2416 1711 (                PRL.  A PRL file is a Page Relocatable file used in Digital Research's multi-) 1711 SB
225 2475 1681 (                user operating system, MP/M.  Under CP/M 3, the CCP handles PRL files) 1681 SB
225 2535 664 (                exactly like COM files.) 664 SB
1154 2830 92 (1-19) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
