225 227 1611 (      When a transient program terminates execution, the BIOS warm start routine) 1611 SB
225 286 1760 (    reloads the CCP into memory.  When the CCP receives control, it tests to see if RSXs) 1760 SB
225 345 1699 (    are resident in memory.  If not, it relocates the LOADER module below the BDOS) 1699 SB
225 405 1759 (    module at the top of the TPA region of memory.  Otherwise, it skips this step because) 1759 SB
225 464 1664 (    the LOADER module is already resident.  The CCP execution cycle then repeats.) 1664 SB
225 582 1684 (      Unlike earlier versions of CP/M, the CCP does not reset the disk system at warm) 1684 SB
225 641 1641 (    start.  However, the CCP does reset the disk system if a CTRL-C is typed at the) 1641 SB
225 700 210 (    prompt.) 210 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 819 806 (    1.6.3 Transient Program Operation) 806 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 937 1633 (      A transient program is one that the CCP loads into the TPA region of memory) 1633 SB
225 996 1724 (    and executes.  As the name transient implies, transient programs are not system resi-) 1724 SB
225 1055 1709 (    dent.  The CCP must load a transient program into memory every time the program) 1709 SB
225 1115 1692 (    is to be executed.  For example, the utilities PIP and RMAC" that are shipped with) 1692 SB
225 1174 1608 (    CP/M 3 execute as transient programs; programs such as word processing and) 1608 SB
225 1233 1661 (    accounting packages distributed by applications vendors also execute as transient) 1661 SB
225 1292 549 (    programs under CP/M 3.) 549 SB
225 1410 1653 (      Section 1.6.2 describes how the CCP prepared the CP/M 3 environment for the) 1653 SB
225 1470 1683 (    execution of a transient program.  To summarize, the CCP initializes Page Zero to) 1683 SB
225 1529 1667 (    contain parsed command-line fields and sets up a 32-byte stack before jumping to) 1667 SB
225 1588 1734 (    location 0100H to pass control to the transient program.  In addition, the CCP might) 1734 SB
225 1647 1738 (    also load RSXs attached to the command file into memory for access by the transient) 1738 SB
225 1706 234 (    program.) 234 SB
225 1825 1671 (      Generally, an executing transient program communicates with the operating sys-) 1671 SB
225 1884 1682 (    tem only through BDOS function calls.  Transient programs make BDOS function) 1682 SB
225 1943 1709 (    calls by loading the CPU registers with the appropriate entry parameters and calling) 1709 SB
225 2002 640 (    location 0005H in Page Zero.) 640 SB
225 2120 1701 (      Transient programs can use BDOS Function 50, Call BIOS, to access BIOS entry) 1701 SB
225 2180 1763 (    points.  This is the preferred method for accessing the BIOS; however, for compatibil-) 1763 SB
225 2239 1735 (    ity with earlier releases of CP/M, transient programs can also make direct BIOS calls) 1735 SB
225 2298 1671 (    for console and list I/O by using the jump instruction at location OOOOH in Page) 1671 SB
225 2357 1755 (    Zero.  But, to simplify portability, use direct BIOS calls only where the primitive level) 1755 SB
225 2416 1732 (    of functionality provided by the BIOS functions is absolutely required.  For example,) 1732 SB
225 2475 1687 (    a disk formatting program must bypass CP/M's disk organization to do its job, and) 1687 SB
225 2535 1745 (    therefore is justified in making direct BIOS calls.  Note however, that disk formatting) 1745 SB
225 2594 629 (    programs are rarely portable.) 629 SB
1154 2830 92 (1-22) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
