SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
0 0 0 fC
225 345 1598 (      A Resident System Extension \(RSX\) is a special type of program that can be) 1598 SB
225 405 1723 (    attached to the operating system to modify or extend the functionality of the BDOS.) 1723 SB
225 464 1743 (    RSX modules intercept BDOS functions and either perform them, translate them into) 1743 SB
225 523 1715 (    other BDOS functions, or pass them through untouched.  The BDOS executes non-) 1715 SB
225 582 945 (    intercepted functions in the standard manner.) 945 SB
225 700 1720 (      A transient program can also use BDOS Function 60, Call Resident System Exten-) 1720 SB
225 760 1724 (    sion, to call an RSX for special functions.  Function 60 is a general purpose function) 1724 SB
225 819 1323 (    that allows customized interfaces between programs and RSXS.) 1323 SB
225 937 1711 (      Two examples of RSX applications are the GET utility and the LOADER module.) 1711 SB
225 996 1716 (    The GET.COM command file has an attached RSX, GET.RSX, which intercepts all) 1716 SB
225 1055 1751 (    console input calls and returns characters from the file specified in the GET command) 1751 SB
225 1115 1709 (    line.  The LOADER module is another example of an RSX, but it is special because) 1709 SB
225 1174 1703 (    it supports Function 59, Load Overlay.  It is always resident in memory when other) 1703 SB
225 1233 385 (    RSXs are active.) 385 SB
225 1351 1739 (      RSXs are loaded into memory at program load time.  As described in Section 1.6.2,) 1739 SB
225 1410 1617 (    after the CCP locates a command file, it calls the LOADER module to load the) 1617 SB
225 1470 1643 (    program into the TPA.  The LOADER loads the transient program into memory) 1643 SB
225 1529 1771 (    along with any attached RSXS.  Subsequently, the loader relocates each attached RSX) 1771 SB
225 1588 1643 (    to the top of the TPA and adjusts the TPA size by changing the jump at location) 1643 SB
225 1647 1747 (    0005H in Page Zero to point to the RSX.  When RSX modules reside in memory, the) 1747 SB
225 1706 1652 (    LOADER module resides directly below the BDOS, and the RSX modules stack) 1652 SB
225 1765 424 (    downward from it.) 424 SB
225 1884 1669 (      The order in which the RSX modules are stacked affects the order in which they) 1669 SB
225 1943 1694 (    intercept BDOS calls.  A more recently stacked RSX has precedence over an older) 1694 SB
225 2002 1694 (    RSX.  Thus,, if two RSXs in memory intercept the same BDOS function, the more) 1694 SB
225 2061 888 (    recently loaded RSX handles the function.) 888 SB
225 2180 1684 (      The CP/M 3 utility GENCOM attaches RSX modules to program files.  Program) 1684 SB
225 2239 1766 (    files with attached RSXs have a special one page header that the LOADER recognizes) 1766 SB
225 2298 1704 (    when it loads the command file.  GENCOM can also attach one or more RSXs to a) 1704 SB
225 2357 1605 (    null command file so that the CCP can load RSXs without having to execute a) 1605 SB
225 2416 1667 (    transient program.  In this case, the command file consists of the RSX header fol-) 1667 SB
225 2475 458 (    lowed by the RSXS.) 458 SB
225 2594 1691 (      RSX modules are Page Relocatable, PRL, files with the file type RSX.  RSX files) 1691 SB
225 2653 1707 (    must be page relocatable because their execution address is determined dynamically) 1707 SB
225 2712 1562 (    by the LOADER module at load time.  RSX files have the following format:) 1562 SB
1154 2830 92 (1-24) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
