225 1256 1094 (                                       Figure 1-8.  RSX File Format) 1094 SB
225 1374 1815 (            RSX files begin with a one page PRL header that specifies the total size of the RSX) 1815 SB
225 1433 1788 (          prefix and code sections.  The PRL bit map is a string of bits identifying those bytes) 1788 SB
225 1493 1704 (          in the RSX prefix and code sections that require relocation.  The PRL format is) 1704 SB
225 1552 1830 (          described in detail in Appendix B. Note that the PRL header and bit map are removed) 1830 SB
225 1611 1782 (          when an RSX is loaded into memory.  They are only used by the LOADER module) 1782 SB
225 1670 466 (          to load the RSX.) 466 SB
225 1788 1708 (            The RSX prefix is a standard data structure that the LOADER module uses to) 1708 SB
225 1848 1805 (          manage RSXs \(see Section 4.4\). Included in this data structure are jump instructions) 1805 SB
225 1907 1727 (          to the previous and next RSX in memory, and two flags.  The LOADER module) 1727 SB
225 1966 1743 (          initializes and updates these 'ump instructions to maintain the link from location 6) 1743 SB
225 2025 1794 (          of Page Zero to the BDOS entry point.  The RSX flags are the Remove flag and the) 1794 SB
225 2084 1788 (          Nonbanked flag.  The Remove flag controls RSX removal from memory.  The CCP) 1788 SB
225 2143 1772 (          tests this flag to determine whether or not it should remove the RSX from memory) 1772 SB
225 2203 1748 (          at system warm start.  The nonbanked flag identifies RSXs that are loaded only in) 1748 SB
225 2262 1743 (          nonbanked CP/M 3 systems.  For example, the CP/M 3 RSX, DIRLBL.RSX, is a) 1743 SB
225 2321 1798 (          nonbanked RSX.  It provides BDOS Function 100, Set Directory Label, support for) 1798 SB
225 2380 1674 (          nonbanked systems only.  Banked systems support this function in the BDOS.) 1674 SB
225 2498 1737 (            The RSX code section contains the main body of the RSX.  This section always) 1737 SB
225 2558 1703 (          begins with code to intercept the BDOS function that is supported by the RSX.) 1703 SB
225 2617 1770 (          Nonintercepted functions are passed to the next RSX in memory.  This section can) 1770 SB
225 2676 1785 (          also include initialization and termination code that transient programs can call with) 1785 SB
225 2735 527 (          BDOS Function 60.) 527 SB
1154 2853 92 (1-25) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
