225 1765 657 (         1.7 System Control Block) 657 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1884 1766 (           The System Control Block, SCB, is a 100 byte CP/M 3 data structure that resides) 1766 SB
225 1943 1762 (         in the BDOS system component.  The SCB contains internal BDOS flags and data,) 1762 SB
225 2002 1803 (         CCP flags and data, and other system information such as console characteristics and) 1803 SB
225 2061 1739 (         the current date and time.  The BDOS, BIOS, CCP system components as well as) 1739 SB
225 2120 1823 (         CP/M 3 utilities and RSXs reference SCB fields.  BDOS Function 49, Get/Set System) 1823 SB
225 2180 1792 (         Control Block, provides access to the SCB fields for transient programs, RSXS, and) 1792 SB
225 2239 298 (         the CCP.) 298 SB
1154 2830 92 (1-27) 92 SB
225 92 1907 (1.6 System Operation                                                                     CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1907 SB
EJ RS
%%PageTrailer
SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 1619 (      However, use caution when you access the SCB through Function 49 for two) 1619 SB
225 286 1668 (    reasons.  First, the SCB is a CP/M 3 data structure.  Digital Research's multi-user) 1668 SB
225 345 1760 (    operating system, MP/M, does not support BDOS Function 49.  Programs that access) 1760 SB
225 405 1738 (    the SCB can run only on CP/M 3. Secondly, the SCB contains critical system param-) 1738 SB
225 464 1770 (    eters that reflect the current state of the operating system.  If a program modifies these) 1770 SB
225 523 1760 (    parameters illegally, the operating system might crash.  However, for application writ-) 1760 SB
225 582 1714 (    ers who are writing system-oriented applications, access to the SCB variables might) 1714 SB
225 641 355 (    prove valuable.) 355 SB
225 760 1703 (      For example, the CCP default drive and current user number are maintained in the) 1703 SB
225 819 1638 (    System Control Block.  This information is displayed in the system prompt.  If a) 1638 SB
225 878 1646 (    transient program changes the current disk or user number by making an explicit) 1646 SB
225 937 1787 (    BDOS call, the System Control Block values are not changed.  They continue to reflect) 1787 SB
225 996 1667 (    the state of the system when the transient program was loaded.  For compatibility) 1667 SB
225 1055 1599 (    with CP/M Version 2, the current disk and user number are also maintained in) 1599 SB
225 1115 1707 (    location 0004H of Page Zero.  The high-order nibble contains the user number, and) 1707 SB
225 1174 832 (    the low-order nibble contains the drive.) 832 SB
225 1292 1714 (      Refer to the description of BDOS Function 49 in Section 2.5 for more information) 1714 SB
225 1351 1653 (    on the System Control Block.  The SCB fields are also discussed in Appendix A.) 1653 SB

