849 362 703 (The BDOS System Interface) 703 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 613 1725 (           This section describes the operating system services available to a transient pro-) 1725 SB
225 672 1752 (         gram through the BDOS module of CP/M 3. The section begins by defining how a) 1752 SB
225 731 1816 (         transient program calls BDOS functions, then discusses serial I/O for console, list and) 1816 SB
225 790 1337 (         auxiliary devices, the file system, and Page Zero initialization.) 1337 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 909 777 (         2.1 BDOS Calling Conventions) 777 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1027 1681 (           CP/M 3 uses a standard convention for BDOS function calls.  On entry to the) 1681 SB
225 1086 1811 (         BDOS, register C contains the BDOS function number, and register pair DE contains) 1811 SB
225 1145 1762 (         a byte or word value or an information address. BDOS functions return single-byte) 1762 SB
225 1204 1712 (         values in register A, and double-byte values in register pair HL. In addition, they) 1712 SB
225 1264 1748 (         return with register A equal to L, and register H equal to B. If a transient program) 1748 SB
225 1323 1784 (         makes a BDOS call to a nonsupported function number in the range of 0 to 127, the) 1784 SB
225 1382 1783 (         BDOS returns with register pair HL set to OFFFFH.  For compatibility with MP/M,) 1783 SB
225 1441 1747 (         the BDOS returns with register pair HL set to OOOOH on nonsupported function) 1747 SB
225 1500 1818 (         numbers in the range of 128 to 255. Note that CP/M 2 returns      with HL set to zero) 1818 SB
225 1559 1774 (         on all invalid function calls.  CP/M 3's register passing conventions for BDOS func-) 1774 SB
225 1619 1739 (         tion calls are consistent with the conventions used by the Intel PL/M systems pro-) 1739 SB
225 1678 519 (         gramming language.) 519 SB
225 1796 1802 (           When a transient program makes a BDOS function call, the BDOS does not restore) 1802 SB
225 1855 1788 (         registers to their entry values before returning to the calling program.  The responsi-) 1788 SB
225 1914 1827 (         bility for saving and restoring any critical re 'ster values rests with the calling program.) 1827 SB
225 2033 1696 (           When the CCP loads a transient program, the LOADER module sets the stack) 1696 SB
225 2092 1847 (         pointer to a 16 level stack, and then pushes the address OOOOH onto the stack.  Thus,) 1847 SB
225 2151 1842 (         an immediate return to the system is equivalent to a jump to OOOOH.  However, most) 1842 SB
225 2210 1692 (         transient programs set up their own stack, and terminate execution by making a) 1692 SB
225 2269 1593 (         BDOS System Reset call \(Function 0\) or by jumping to location OOOOH.) 1593 SB
1166 2861 67 (2-1) 67 SB
