225 1351 634 (         2.2.1 BDOS Console I/O) 634 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1470 1786 (           Because a transient program's main interaction with its user is through the console,) 1786 SB
225 1529 1837 (         the BDOS supports many console I/O functions.  Console PO functions can be divided) 1837 SB
225 1588 1740 (         into four categories: basic console I/O, direct console I/O, buffered console input,) 1740 SB
225 1647 1769 (         and special console functions.  Using the basic console I/O functions, programs can) 1769 SB
225 1706 1762 (         access the console device for simple input and output.  The basic console I/O func-) 1762 SB
225 1765 301 (         tions are:) 301 SB
225 1884 566 (                 1. Console Input ) 566 SB
825 1884 513 (- Inputs a single character) 513 SB
225 1943 588 (                 2. Console Output) 588 SB
825 1943 548 (- Outputs a single character) 548 SB
225 2002 567 (                 9. Print String     ) 567 SB
825 2002 619 (- Outputs a string of characters) 619 SB
225 2061 596 (                11. Console Status ) 596 SB
825 2061 794 (- Signals if a character is ready for input) 794 SB
225 2120 1217 (              111. Print Block       - Outputs a block of characters) 1217 SB
225 2239 1779 (           The input function echoes the character to the console so that the user can identify) 1779 SB
225 2298 1820 (         the typed character.  The output functions expand tabs in columns of eight characters,) 1820 SB
1166 2830 67 (2-3) 67 SB
225 92 1885 (2.2 BDOS Serial Device I/O                                                         CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 1885 SB
