225 227 1693 (           Function 10 also filters input for certain control characters.  If the user types a) 1693 SB
225 286 1795 (         CTRL-C as the first character in the line, Function 10 terminates the calling program) 1795 SB
225 345 1765 (         by branching to the BIOS warm start entry point.  A CTRL-C in any other position) 1765 SB
225 405 1784 (         is simply echoed at the console.  Function 10 also watches for a CTRL-P keystroke,) 1784 SB
225 464 1713 (         and if it finds one at any position in the command line, it toggles the printer echo) 1713 SB
225 523 1738 (         switch.  Function 10 does not filter CTRL-S and CTRL-Q characters, but accepts) 1738 SB
225 582 1736 (         them as normal input.  In general, all control characters that Function 10 does not) 1736 SB
225 641 1754 (         recognize as editing control characters, it accepts as input characters.  Function 10) 1754 SB
225 700 1665 (         identifies a control character with a leading caret, ', when it echoes the control) 1665 SB
225 760 1747 (         character to the console.  Thus, CTRL-C appears as 'C in a Function 10 command) 1747 SB
225 819 476 (         line on the screen.) 476 SB
225 937 1752 (           The final category of console I/O functions includes special functions that modify) 1752 SB
225 996 1332 (         the behavior of other console functions.  These functions are:) 1332 SB
225 1115 753 (              109.  Get/Set Console Mode) 753 SB
225 1174 800 (              110.  Get/Set Output Delimiter) 800 SB
225 1292 1746 (         Function 110 can get or set the current delimiter for Function 9, Print String.  The) 1746 SB
225 1351 1789 (         delimiter is $, when a transient program begins execution.  Function 109 gets or sets) 1789 SB
225 1410 1750 (         a 16-bit system variable called the Console Mode.  The following list describes the) 1750 SB
225 1470 1184 (         bits of the Console Mode variable and their functions:) 1184 SB
225 1588 1712 (         bit 0    If this bit is set, Function 11 returns true only if a CTRL-C is typed at the) 1712 SB
225 1647 1781 (                  console.  Programs that make repeated console status calls to test if execution) 1781 SB
225 1706 1672 (                  should be interrupted, can set this bit to interrupt on CTRL-C only.  The) 1672 SB
225 1765 1376 (                  CCP DIR and TYPE built-in commands run in this mode.) 1376 SB
225 1884 1704 (         bit 1    Setting this bit disables stop and start scroll support for the basic console) 1704 SB
225 1943 1690 (                  110 functions, which comprise the first category of functions described in) 1690 SB
225 2002 1717 (                  this section.  When this bit is set, Function 1 reads CTRL-S, CTRL-Q, and) 1717 SB
225 2061 1784 (                  CTRL-P, and Function 1 1 returns true if the user types these characters.  Use) 1784 SB
225 2120 1750 (                  this mode in situations where raw console input and edited output is needed.) 1750 SB
225 2180 1699 (                  While in this mode, you can use Function 6 for input and input status, and) 1699 SB
225 2239 1641 (                  Functions 1, 9, and 111 for output without the possibility of the output) 1641 SB
225 2298 1651 (                  functions intercepting input CTRL-S, CTRL-Q, or CTRL-P characters.) 1651 SB
225 2416 1779 (         bit 2 :  Setting this bit disables tab expansion and printer echo support for Functions) 1779 SB
225 2475 1527 (                  2, 9, and 111. Use this mode when non-edited output is required.) 1527 SB
1166 2830 67 (2-5) 67 SB
