225 227 600 (          2.3 BDOS File System) 600 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 1773 (            Transient programs depend on the BDOS file system to create, update, and main-) 1773 SB
225 405 1848 (          tain disk files.  This section describes the capabilities of the BDOS file system in detail.) 1848 SB
225 464 1771 (          You must understand the general features of CP/M 3 described in Section 1 before) 1771 SB
225 523 1061 (          you can use the detail presented in this section.) 1061 SB
225 641 1724 (            The remaining introductory paragraphs define the four categories of BDOS file) 1724 SB
225 700 1781 (          functions.  This is followed by a review of file naming conventions and disk and file) 1781 SB
225 760 1771 (          organization.  The section then describes the data structure used by the BDOS file,) 1771 SB
225 819 1795 (          and directory oriented functions: the File Control Block \(FCB\).  Subsequent discus-) 1795 SB
225 878 1774 (          sions cover file attributes, user numbers, directory labels and extended File Control) 1774 SB
225 937 1835 (          Blocks \(XFCBs\), passwords, date and time stamping, blocking and deblocking, multi-) 1835 SB
225 996 1781 (          sector I/O, disk reset and removable media, byte counts, and error handling.  These) 1781 SB
225 1055 1746 (          topics are closely related to the BDOS file system.  You must be familiar with the) 1746 SB
225 1115 1828 (          contents of Section 2 before attempting to use the BDOS functions described individ-) 1828 SB
225 1174 486 (          ually in Section 3.) 486 SB
225 1292 1780 (            The BDOS file system supports four categories of functions: file access functions,) 1780 SB
225 1351 1742 (          directory functions, drive related functions, and miscellaneous functions.  The file) 1742 SB
225 1410 1774 (          access category includes functions to create a file, open an existing file, and close a) 1774 SB
225 1470 1728 (          file.  Both the make and open functions activate the file for subsequent access by) 1728 SB
225 1529 1829 (          BDOS file access functions.  The BDOS read and write functions are file access func-) 1829 SB
225 1588 1780 (          tions that operate either sequentially or randomly by record position.  They transfer) 1780 SB
225 1647 1813 (          data in units of 128 bytes, which is the basic record size of the file system.  The close) 1813 SB
225 1706 1728 (          function makes any necessary updates to the directory to permanently record the) 1728 SB
225 1765 637 (          status of an activated file.) 637 SB
1166 2830 67 (2-7) 67 SB
225 92 452 (2.3 BDOS File System) 452 SB
1597 92 578 (CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 578 SB
EJ RS
