225 227 1679 (          Table 2-2 displays the relationship between data block size and drive capacity.) 1679 SB
225 345 1080 (                              Table 2-2.  Logical Drive Capacity) 1080 SB
225 464 1264 (                      Data Block Size           Maximum Drive Capacity) 1264 SB
225 582 1025 (                              1K                       256 Kilobytes) 1025 SB
225 641 1041 (                              2K                        64 Megabytes) 1041 SB
225 700 1053 (                              4K                       128 Megabytes) 1053 SB
225 760 1040 (                              8K                      256 Megabytes) 1040 SB
225 819 1052 (                             16K                      512 Megabytes) 1052 SB
225 937 1694 (      Logical drives are divided into two regions: a directory area and a data area.  The) 1694 SB
225 996 1657 (    directory area contains from one to sixteen blocks located at the beginning of the) 1657 SB
225 1055 1688 (    drive.  The actual number is set in the BIOS.  This area contains entries that define) 1688 SB
225 1115 1732 (    which files exist on the drive.  The directory entries corresponding to a particular file) 1732 SB
225 1174 1676 (    define those data blocks in the drive's data area that belong to the file.  These data) 1676 SB
225 1233 1702 (    blocks contain the file's records.  The directory area is logically subdivided into six-) 1702 SB
225 1292 1746 (    teen independent directories identified as user 0 through 15.  Each independent direc-) 1746 SB
225 1351 1719 (    tory shares the actual directory area on the drive.  However, a file's directory entries) 1719 SB
225 1410 1674 (    cannot exist under more than one user number.  In general, only files belonging to) 1674 SB
225 1470 1066 (    the current user number are visible in the directory.) 1066 SB
225 1588 1698 (      Each disk file consists of a set of up to 262,144 128-byte records.  Each record in) 1698 SB
225 1647 1753 (    a file is identified by its position in the file.  This position is called the record's random) 1753 SB
225 1706 1743 (    record number.  If a file is created sequentially, the first record has a position of zero,) 1743 SB
225 1765 1669 (    while the last record has a position one less than the number of records in the file.) 1669 SB
225 1825 1738 (    Such a file can be read sequentially in record position order beginning at record zero,) 1738 SB
225 1884 1740 (    or randomly by record position.  Conversely, if a file is created randomly, records are) 1740 SB
225 1943 1673 (    added to the file by specified position.  A file created in this way is called sparse if) 1673 SB
225 2002 1358 (    positions exist within the file where a record has not been written.) 1358 SB
225 2120 1660 (      The BDOS automatically allocates data blocks to a file to contain its records on) 1660 SB
225 2180 1649 (    the basis of the record positions consumed.  Thus, a sparse file that contains two) 1649 SB
225 2239 1727 (    records, one at position zero, the other at position 262,143, consumes only two data) 1727 SB
225 2298 1717 (    blocks in the data area.  Sparse files can only be created and accessed randomly, not) 1717 SB
225 2357 1712 (    sequentially.  Note that any data block allocated to a file is permanently allocated to) 1712 SB
225 2416 1758 (    the file until the file is deleted or truncated.  These are the only mechanisms supported) 1758 SB
225 2475 1199 (    by the BDOS for releasing data blocks belonging to a file.) 1199 SB
1154 2830 92 (2-12) 92 SB
225 92 452 (2.3 BDOS File System) 452 SB
