225 227 1791 (          Source files under CP/M 3      are treated as a sequence of ASCII characters, where) 1791 SB
225 286 1766 (        each line of the source file is followed by a carriage return line-feed sequence, ODH) 1766 SB
225 345 1821 (        followed by OAH.  Thus a single 128-byte record could contain several lines of source) 1821 SB
225 405 1800 (        text.  The end of an ASCII file is denoted by a CTRL-Z character, 1AH, or a real end) 1800 SB
225 464 1772 (        of file, returned by the BDOS read operation.  CTRL-Z characters embedded within) 1772 SB
225 523 1748 (        machine code files such as COM files are ignored.  The actual end-of-file condition) 1748 SB
225 582 1285 (        returned by the BDOS is used to terminate read operations.) 1285 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 700 836 (        2.3.3 File Control Block Definition) 836 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 819 1735 (          The File Control Block, FCB, is a data structure that is set up and initialized by a) 1735 SB
225 878 1727 (        transient program, and then used by any BDOS file access and directory functions) 1727 SB
225 937 1762 (        called by the transient program.  Thus the FCB is an important channel for informa-) 1762 SB
225 996 1714 (        tion exchange between the BDOS and a transient program.  For example, when a) 1714 SB
225 1055 1782 (        program opens a file, and subsequently accesses it with BDOS read and write record) 1782 SB
225 1115 1742 (        functions, the BDOS file system maintains the current file state and position within) 1742 SB
225 1174 1802 (        the program's FCB.  Some BDOS functions use certain fields in the FCB for invoking) 1802 SB
225 1233 1705 (        special options.  Other BDOS functions use the FCB to return data to the calling) 1705 SB
225 1292 1715 (        program.  In addition, all BDOS random I/O functions specify the random record) 1715 SB
225 1351 1081 (        number with a 3-byte field at the end of the FCB.) 1081 SB
225 1470 1696 (          When a transient program makes a file access or directory BDOS function call,) 1696 SB
225 1529 1774 (        register pair DE must address an FCB.  The length of the FCB data area depends on) 1774 SB
225 1588 1797 (        the BDOS function.  For most functions, the required length is 33 bytes.  For random) 1797 SB
225 1647 1719 (        I/O functions, the Truncate File function, and the Compute File Size function, the) 1719 SB
225 1706 1587 (        FCB length must be 36 bytes.  The FCB format is shown on the next page.) 1587 SB
1154 2830 92 (2-13) 92 SB
225 92 452 (2.3 BDOS File System) 452 SB
1597 92 578 (CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 578 SB
