225 227 746 (     2.3.5 User Number Conventions) 746 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 1684 (       The CP/M 3 User facility divides each drive directory into sixteen logically inde-) 1684 SB
225 405 1729 (     pendent directories, designated as user 0 through user 15.  Physically, all user direc-) 1729 SB
225 464 1702 (     tories share the directory area of a drive.  In most other aspects, however, they are) 1702 SB
225 523 1743 (     independent.  For example, files with the same name can exist on different user num-) 1743 SB
225 582 1698 (     bers of the same drive with no conflict.  However, a single file cannot reside under) 1698 SB
225 641 621 (     more than one user number.) 621 SB
225 760 1647 (       Only one user number is active for a program at one time, and the current user) 1647 SB
225 819 1703 (     number applies to all drives on the system.  Furthermore, the FCB format does not) 1703 SB
225 878 1707 (     contain any field that can be used to override the current user number.  As a result,) 1707 SB
225 937 1742 (     all file and directory operations reference directories associated with the current user) 1742 SB
225 996 1734 (     number.  However, it is possible for a program to access files on different user num-) 1734 SB
225 1055 1688 (     bers; this can be accomplished by setting the user number to the file's user number) 1688 SB
225 1115 1733 (     with the BDOS Set User function before making the desired BDOS function call for) 1733 SB
225 1174 1780 (     the file.  Note that this technique must be used carefully.  An error occurs if a program) 1780 SB
225 1233 1599 (     attempts to read or write to a file under a user number different from the user) 1599 SB
225 1292 1050 (     number that was active when the file was opened.) 1050 SB
225 1410 1709 (       When the CCP loads and executes a transient program, it initializes the user num-) 1709 SB
225 1470 1635 (     ber to the value displayed in the system prompt.  If the system prompt does not) 1635 SB
225 1529 1734 (     display a user number, user zero is implied.  A transient program can change its user) 1734 SB
225 1588 1747 (     number by making a BDOS Set User function call.  Changing the user number in this) 1747 SB
225 1647 1701 (     way does not affect the CCP's user number displayed in the system prompt.  When) 1701 SB
225 1706 1702 (     the transient program terminates, the CCP's user number is restored.  However, an) 1702 SB
225 1765 1709 (     option of the BDOS Program Chain command allows a program to pass its current) 1709 SB
225 1825 1143 (     user number and default drive to the chained program.) 1143 SB
225 1943 1708 (       User 0 has special properties under CP/M 3. When the current user number is not) 1708 SB
225 2002 1681 (     equal to zero, and if a requested file is not present under the current user number,) 1681 SB
225 2061 1662 (     the file system automatically attempts to open the file under user zero.  If the file) 1662 SB
225 2120 1673 (     exists under user zero, and if it has the system attribute, t2', set, the file is opened) 1673 SB
225 2180 1695 (     from user zero.  Note, however, that files opened in this way cannot be written to;) 1695 SB
225 2239 1635 (     they are available only for read access.  This procedure allows utilities that may) 1635 SB
225 2298 1693 (     include overlays and any other commonly accessed files to be placed on user zero,) 1693 SB
225 2357 1661 (     but also be available for access from other user numbers.  As a result, commonly) 1661 SB
225 2416 1695 (     needed utilities need not be copied to all user numbers on a directory, and you can) 1695 SB
