225 227 1785 (         Only one Directory Label can exist in a drive's directory.  The Directory Label name) 1785 SB
225 286 1693 (         and type fields are not used to search for a Directory Label; they can be used to) 1693 SB
225 345 1710 (         identify a disk.  A Directory Label can be created, or its fields can be updated by) 1710 SB
225 405 1777 (         BDOS function 100, Set Directory Label.  This function can also assign a Directory) 1777 SB
225 464 1725 (         Label a password.  The Directory Label password, if assigned, cannot be circum-) 1725 SB
225 523 1725 (         vented, whereas file password protection is an option controlled by the Directory) 1725 SB
225 582 1765 (         Label.  Thus, access to the Directory Label password provides a kind of super-user) 1765 SB
225 641 510 (         status on that drive.) 510 SB
225 760 1748 (           The nonbanked version of CP/M 3 does not support file passwords.  However, it) 1748 SB
225 819 1886 (         does provide password protection of directory labels.  The CP/M 3 RSX, DIRLBL.RSX,) 1886 SB
225 878 1739 (         which implements BDOS Function 100 in the nonbanked version of CP/M 3, pro-) 1739 SB
225 937 480 (         vides this support.) 480 SB
225 1055 1738 (           The BDOS file system has no function to read the Directory Label FCB directly.) 1738 SB
225 1115 1815 (         However, the Directory Label data byte can be read directly with the BDOS Function) 1815 SB
225 1174 1815 (         101, Return Directory Label.  In addition, the BDOS Search functions, with a ? in the) 1815 SB
225 1233 1773 (         FCB drive byte, can be used to find the Directory Label on the default drive.  In the) 1773 SB
225 1292 1763 (         directory, the Directory Label is identified by a drive byte value, byte 0 in the FCB,) 1763 SB
225 1351 465 (         equal to 32, 20H.) 465 SB
