225 641 998 (              2.3.9 Record Blocking and Deblocking) 998 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 760 1798 (               Under CP/M 3, the logical record size for disk I/O is 128 bytes.  This is the basic) 1798 SB
225 819 1877 (              unit of data transfer between the operating system and transient programs.  However,) 1877 SB
225 878 1853 (              on disk, the record size is not restricted to 128 bytes.  These records, called physical) 1853 SB
225 937 1876 (              records, can range from 128 bytes to 4K bytes in size.  Record blocking and deblock-) 1876 SB
225 996 1828 (              ing is required on systems that support drives with physical record sizes larger than) 1828 SB
225 1055 386 (              128 bytes.) 386 SB
225 1174 1827 (               The process of building up physical records from 128 byte logical records is called) 1827 SB
225 1233 1878 (              record blocking.  This process is required in write operations.  The reverse process of) 1878 SB
225 1292 1838 (              breaking up physical records into their component 128 byte logical records is called) 1838 SB
225 1351 1908 (              record deblocking.  This process is required in read operations.  Under CP/M 3, record) 1908 SB
225 1410 1408 (              blocking and deblocking is normally performed by the BDOS.) 1408 SB
225 1529 1747 (               Record deblocking implies a read-ahead operation.  For example, if a transient) 1747 SB
225 1588 1763 (              program makes a BDOS function call to read a logical record that resides at the) 1763 SB
225 1647 1756 (              beginning of a physical record, the entire physical record is read into an internal) 1756 SB
225 1706 1784 (              buffer.  Subsequent BDOS read calls for the remaining logical records access the) 1784 SB
225 1765 1831 (              buffer instead of the disk.  Conversely, record blocking results in the postponement) 1831 SB
225 1825 1784 (              of physical write operations but only for data write operations.  For example, if a) 1784 SB
225 1884 1807 (              ransient program makes a BDOS write call, the logical record is placed in a buffer) 1807 SB
225 1943 1839 (              equal in size to the physical record size.  The write operation on the physical record) 1839 SB
225 2002 197 (              t) 197 SB
225 2061 1788 (              buffer is postponed until the buffer is needed in another I/O operation.  Note that) 1788 SB
225 2120 1435 (              under CP/M 3, directory write operations are never postponed.) 1435 SB
225 2239 1835 (               Postponing physical record write operations has implications for some applications) 1835 SB
225 2298 1837 (              programs.  For those programs that involve file updating, it is often critical to guar-) 1837 SB
225 2357 1801 (              antee that the state of the file on disk parallels the state of the file in memory after) 1801 SB
225 2416 1821 (              the update operation.  This is only an issue on systems where physical write opera-) 1821 SB
225 2475 1871 (              tions are postponed because of record blocking and deblocking.  If the system should) 1871 SB
225 2535 1832 (              crash while a physical buffer is pending, data would be lost.  To prevent this loss of) 1832 SB
225 2594 1845 (              data, the BDOS Flush Buffers function, function 48, can be called to force the write) 1845 SB
225 2653 810 (              of any pending physical buffers.) 810 SB
1154 2830 92 (2-25) 92 SB
