225 641 548 (    2.3.10 Multi-Sector I/O) 548 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 760 1642 (      CP/M 3 can read or write multiple 128-byte records in a single BDOS function) 1642 SB
225 819 1702 (    call.  This process, called multi-sector I/O, is useful primarily in sequential read and) 1702 SB
225 878 1655 (    write operations, particularly on drives with physical record sizes larger than 128) 1655 SB
225 937 1762 (    bytes.  In a multi-sector I/O operation, the BDOS file system bypasses, when possible,) 1762 SB
225 996 1700 (    all intermediate record buffering.  Data is transferred directly between the TPA and) 1700 SB
225 1055 1650 (    the drive.  In addition, the BDOS informs the BIOS when it is reading or writing) 1650 SB
225 1115 1728 (    multiple physical records in sequence on a drive.  The BIOS can use this information) 1728 SB
225 1174 1734 (    to further optimize the I/O operation resulting in even better performance.  Thus, the) 1734 SB
225 1233 1726 (    primary objective of multi-sector I/O is to improve sequential I/O performance.  The) 1726 SB
225 1292 1735 (    actual improvement obtained, however, depends on the hardware environment of the) 1735 SB
225 1351 1041 (    host system, and the implementation of the BIOS.) 1041 SB
225 1470 1693 (      The number of records that can be supported with multi-sector I/O ranges from 1) 1693 SB
225 1529 1673 (    to 128.  This value can be set by BDOS function 44, Set multi-sector Count.  The) 1673 SB
225 1588 1766 (    multi-sector count is set to one when a transient program begins execution.  However,) 1766 SB
225 1647 1748 (    the CP/M 3 LOADER module executes with the multi-sector Count set to 128 unless) 1748 SB
225 1706 1669 (    the available TPA space is less than 16K.  In addition, the CP/M 3 PIP utility also) 1669 SB
225 1765 1743 (    sets the multi-sector count to 128 when sufficient buffer space is available.  Note that) 1743 SB
225 1825 1750 (    the greatest potential performance increases are obtained when the multi-sector count) 1750 SB
225 1884 1073 (    is set to 128.  Of course, this requires a 16K buffer.) 1073 SB
225 2002 1663 (      The multi-sector count determines the number of operations to be performed by) 1663 SB
225 2061 669 (    the following BDOS functions:) 669 SB
225 2180 882 (         o Sequential Read and Write functions) 882 SB
225 2239 1625 (         o Random Read and Write functions including Write Random with Zero Fill) 1625 SB
225 2357 1662 (      If the multi-sector count is N, calling one of the above functions is equivalent to) 1662 SB
225 2416 1709 (    making N function calls.  If a multi-sector I/O operation is interrupted with an error) 1709 SB
225 2475 1667 (    such as reading unwritten data, the file system returns in register H the number of) 1667 SB
225 2535 860 (    128-byte records successfully processed.) 860 SB
1154 2830 92 (2-26) 92 SB
225 92 452 (2.3 BDOS File System) 452 SB
1597 92 578 (CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 578 SB
