225 227 962 (        2.3.11 Disk Reset and Removable Media) 962 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 1789 (          The BDOS functions, Disk Reset \(function 13\) and Reset Drive \(function 37\) allow) 1789 SB
225 405 1762 (        a program to control when a disk's directory is to be reinitialized for file operations.) 1762 SB
225 464 1701 (        This process of initializing a disk's directory is called logging-in the drive.  When) 1701 SB
225 523 1742 (        CP/M 3 is cold started, all drives are in the reset state.  Subsequently, as drives are) 1742 SB
225 582 1723 (        referenced, they are automatically logged-in by the file system.  Once logged-in, a) 1723 SB
225 641 1670 (        drive remains in the logged-in state until it is reset by BDOS function 13 or 37.) 1670 SB
225 700 1737 (        Following the reset operation, the drive is again automatically logged-in by the file) 1737 SB
225 760 1814 (        system when it is next used.  Note that BDOS functions 13 and 37 have similar effects) 1814 SB
225 819 1741 (        except that function 13 is directed to all drives on the system.  Any combination of) 1741 SB
225 878 835 (        drives can be reset with Function 37.) 835 SB
225 996 1780 (          Logging-in a drive consists of several steps.  The most important step is the initiali-) 1780 SB
225 1055 1740 (        zation of the drive's allocation vector.  The allocation vector records the allocation) 1740 SB
225 1115 1730 (        and deallocation of data blocks to files, as files are created, extended, deleted, and) 1730 SB
225 1174 1759 (        truncated, Another function performed during drive log-in is the initialization of the) 1759 SB
225 1233 1842 (        directory check-sum vector.  The file system uses the check-sum vector to detect media) 1842 SB
225 1292 1799 (        changes on a drive.  Note that permanent drives, which are drives that do not support) 1799 SB
225 1351 1708 (        media changes, might not have check-sum vectors.  If directory hashing has been) 1708 SB
225 1410 1755 (        specified for the drive, a BIOS and GENCPM option, the file system creates a hash) 1755 SB
225 1470 819 (        table for the directory during log-in.) 819 SB
225 1588 1744 (          The primary use of the drive reset functions is to prepare for a media change on a) 1744 SB
225 1647 1798 (        drive.  Subsequently, when the drive is accessed by a BDOS function call, the drive is) 1798 SB
225 1706 1807 (        automatically logged-in.  Resetting a drive has two important side effects.  First of all,) 1807 SB
225 1765 1775 (        any pending blocking/deblocking buffers on the reset drive are discarded.  Secondly,) 1775 SB
225 1825 1736 (        any data blocks that have been allocated to files that have not been closed are lost.) 1736 SB
225 1884 1789 (        An application program should close files, particularly files that have been written to,) 1789 SB
225 1943 600 (        prior to resetting a drive.) 600 SB
225 2061 1731 (           Although CP/M 3 automatically relogs in removable media when media changes) 1731 SB
225 2120 1659 (        are detected, the application program should still explicitly reset a drive before) 1659 SB
225 2180 812 (        prompting the user to change disks.) 812 SB
