225 227 547 (    2.3.12 File Byte Counts) 547 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 1725 (      Although the logical record size of CP/M 3 is restricted to 128 bytes, CP/M 3 does) 1725 SB
225 405 1673 (    provide a mechanism to store and retrieve a byte count for a file.  This facility can) 1673 SB
225 464 1607 (    identify the last byte of the last record of a file.  The BDOS Compute File Size) 1607 SB
225 523 1594 (    function returns the random record number, plus 1, of the last record of a file.) 1594 SB
225 641 1709 (      The BDOS Set File Attributes function can set a file's byte count.  Conversely, the) 1709 SB
225 700 1682 (    Open function can return a file's byte count to the cr field of the FCB.  The BDOS) 1682 SB
225 760 1659 (    Search and Search Next functions also return a file's byte count.  These functions) 1659 SB
225 819 1685 (    return the byte count in the sl field of the FCB returned in the current DMA buffer) 1685 SB
225 878 921 (    \(see BDOS Functions Returned 17 and 26\).) 921 SB
225 996 1723 (      Note that the file system does not access or update the byte count value in file read) 1723 SB
225 1055 1744 (    or write operations.  However, the BDOS Make File function does set the byte count) 1744 SB
225 1115 1104 (    of a file to zero when it creates a file in the directory.) 1104 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 1233 675 (    2.3.13 BDOS Error Handling) 675 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1351 1509 (      The BDOS file system responds to error situations in one of three ways:) 1509 SB
225 1470 1700 (        Method 1.             It returns to the calling program with return codes  in register) 1700 SB
225 1529 390 (                              ) 390 SB
675 1529 684 (  A, H, and L identifying the error.) 684 SB
225 1647 1671 (        Method 2.             It displays an error message on the console, and branches to) 1671 SB
225 1706 390 (                              ) 390 SB
675 1706 1225 (  the BIOS warm start entry point, thereby terminating execu-) 1225 SB
225 1765 390 (                              ) 390 SB
675 1765 568 (  tion of the calling program.) 568 SB
225 1884 1636 (        Method 3.             It displays an error message on the console, and returns to) 1636 SB
225 1943 390 (                              ) 390 SB
675 1943 727 (  the calling program as in method 1.) 727 SB
225 2061 1742 (    The file system handles   the majority of errors it detects by method 1. Two examples) 1742 SB
225 2120 1730 (    of this kind of error are the file not found error for the open function and the reading) 1730 SB
225 2180 1752 (    unwritten data error for a read function.  More serious errors, such as disk I/O errors,) 1752 SB
225 2239 1750 (    are usually handled by method 2. Errors in this category, called physical and extended) 1750 SB
225 2298 1474 (    errors, can also be reported by methods 1 and 3 under program control.) 1474 SB
1154 2830 92 (2-28) 92 SB
225 92 452 (2.3 BDOS File System) 452 SB
1597 92 578 (CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 578 SB
