225 227 1400 (                         BDOS FUNCTION 36: SET RANDOM RECORD) 1400 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 670 (                        Entry Parameters:) 670 SB
225 405 733 (                              Registers C: 24H) 733 SB
225 464 921 (                                           DE: FCB Address) 921 SB
225 582 1166 (                        Returned Value: Random Record Field Set) 1166 SB
225 760 1719 (          The Set Random Record function returns the random record number of the next) 1719 SB
225 819 1665 (        record to be accessed from a file that has been read or written sequentially to a) 1665 SB
225 878 1750 (        particular point.  This value is returned in the random record field, bytes rO, rl, and) 1750 SB
225 937 1754 (        r2, of the FCB addressed by the register pair DE.  Function 36 can be useful in two) 1754 SB
225 996 217 (        ways,) 217 SB
225 1115 1727 (          First, it is often necessary to initially read and scan a sequential file to extract the) 1727 SB
225 1174 1765 (        positions of various key fields.  As each key is encountered, Function 36 is called to) 1765 SB
225 1233 1732 (        compute the random record position for the data corresponding to this key.  If the) 1732 SB
225 1292 1713 (        data unit size is 128 bytes, the resulting record number minus one is placed into a) 1713 SB
225 1351 1734 (        table with the key for later retrieval.  After scanning the entire file and tabularizing) 1734 SB
225 1410 1734 (        the keys and their record numbers, you can move directly to a particular record by) 1734 SB
225 1470 1755 (        performing a random read using the corresponding random record number that you) 1755 SB
225 1529 1885 (        saved earlier.  The scheme is easily generalized when variable record lengths are involved,) 1885 SB
225 1588 1765 (        because the program need only store the buffer-relative byte position along with the) 1765 SB
225 1647 1772 (        key and record number to find the exact starting position of the keyed data at a later) 1772 SB
225 1706 203 (        time.) 203 SB
225 1825 1674 (          A second use of Function 36 occurs when switching from a sequential read or) 1674 SB
225 1884 1711 (        write over to random read or write.  A file is sequentially accessed to a particular) 1711 SB
225 1943 1768 (        point in the file, then Function 36 is called to set the record number, and subsequent) 1768 SB
225 2002 1589 (        random read and write operations continue from the next record in the file.) 1589 SB
1154 2830 92 (3-55) 92 SB
225 92 861 (BDOS Function Calls                                 ) 861 SB
1605 92 570 (CP/M 3 Programmers Guide) 570 SB
