225 603 1728 (          The programs presented in this section illustrate how to use the BDOS functions) 1728 SB
225 662 1718 (        described in the previous section.  The examples show how to copy a file, how to) 1718 SB
225 721 1765 (        dump a file, how to create or access a random access file, and how to write an RSX) 1765 SB
225 780 286 (        program.) 286 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 899 977 (        4.1 A Sample File-To-File Copy Program) 977 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1017 1740 (          The following program illustrates simple file operations.  You can create the pro-) 1740 SB
225 1076 1647 (        gram source file, COPY.ASM, using ED or another editor, and then assemble) 1647 SB
225 1135 1659 (        COPY.ASM using MAC.  MAC produces the file COPY.HEX. Use the utility) 1659 SB
225 1194 1574 (        HEXCOM to produce a COPY.COM file that can execute under CP/M 3.) 1574 SB
225 1313 1676 (          The COPY program first sets the stack pointer to a local area, then moves the) 1676 SB
225 1372 1741 (        second name from the default area at 006CH to a 33-byte file control block named) 1741 SB
225 1431 1787 (        DFCB.  The DFCB is then prepared for file operations by clearing the current record) 1787 SB
225 1490 1758 (        field.  Because the CCP sets up the source FCB at 005CH upon entry to the COPY) 1758 SB
225 1549 1791 (        program, the source and destination FCBs are now ready for processing.  To prepare) 1791 SB
225 1609 1778 (        the source FCB, the CCP places the first name into the default FCB, with the proper) 1778 SB
225 1668 1253 (        fields zeroed, including the current record field at 007CH.) 1253 SB
225 1786 1735 (          COPY continues by opening the source file, deleting any existing destination file,) 1735 SB
225 1845 1730 (        and then creating the destination file.  If each of these operations is successful, the) 1730 SB
225 1904 1730 (        COPY program loops at the label COPY until each record is read from the source) 1730 SB
225 1964 1717 (        file and placed into the destination file.  Upon completion of the data transfer, the) 1717 SB
225 2023 1688 (        destination file is closed, and the program returns to the CCP command level by) 1688 SB
225 2082 480 (        lumping to BOOT.) 480 SB
1166 2851 67 (4-1) 67 SB
EJ RS
