0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 871 (   4.3 A Sample Random Access Program) 871 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 345 1648 (     This example is an extensive but complete example of random access operation.) 1648 SB
225 405 1633 (   The following program reads or writes random records upon command from the) 1633 SB
225 464 1617 (   terminal.  When the program has been created, assembled, and placed into a file) 1617 SB
225 523 1042 (   labeled RANDOM.COM, the CCP level command) 1042 SB
225 641 486 (   A>RANDOM X.DAT) 486 SB
225 760 1612 (   can start the test program.  In this case, the RANDOM program looks for a file) 1612 SB
225 819 1626 (   X.DAT and, if it finds it, prompts the console for input.  If X.DAT is not found,) 1626 SB
225 878 1680 (   RANDOM creates the      file before displaying the prompt. Each prompt takes the) 1680 SB
225 937 148 (   form:) 148 SB
225 1055 416 (        next command?) 416 SB
225 1174 1680 (   and is followed by operator input, terminated by a carriage return.  The input com-) 1680 SB
225 1233 459 (   mands take the form:) 459 SB
225 1351 357 (        nW nR nF Q) 357 SB
225 1470 1697 (   where n is an integer value in the range 0 to 262143, and W, R, F, and Q are simple) 1697 SB
225 1529 1661 (   command characters corresponding to random write, W, random read, R, random) 1661 SB
225 1588 1672 (   write with zero fill, F, and quit processing, Q. If you enter a W or F command, the) 1672 SB
225 1647 816 (   RANDOM program issues the prompt:) 816 SB
225 1765 235 (   type data:) 235 SB
225 1884 1625 (   You then respond by typing up to 127 characters, followed by a carriage return.) 1625 SB
225 1943 1662 (   RANDOM then writes the character string into the X.DAT file at record n. If you) 1662 SB
225 2002 1725 (   enter an F command, the RANDOM program fills previously unallocated data blocks) 1725 SB
225 2061 1665 (   with zeros before writing record n. If you enter the R command, RANDOM reads) 1665 SB
225 2120 1620 (   record number n and displays the string value at the console.  If you enter the Q) 1620 SB
225 2180 1652 (   command, the X.DAT file is closed, and the program returns to the console com-) 1652 SB
225 2239 1404 (   mand processor.  In the interest of brevity, the only error message is:) 1404 SB
225 2357 320 (   err  , try again) 320 SB
1154 2830 92 (4-10) 92 SB
225 92 1047 (4.3 A Sample Random Access Program                    ) 1047 SB
1597 92 578 (CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 578 SB
EJ RS
