225 1470 1759 (          You could make the following major improvements to this program to enhance its) 1759 SB
225 1529 1682 (         operation.  With some work, this program could evolve into a simple data base) 1682 SB
225 1588 1784 (         management system.  You could, for example, assume a standard record size of 128) 1784 SB
225 1647 1785 (         bytes, consisting of arbitrary fields within the record.  You could develop a program) 1785 SB
225 1706 1770 (         called GETKEY that first reads a sequential file and extracts a specific field defined) 1770 SB
225 1765 1000 (         by the operator.  For example, the command) 1000 SB
225 1884 1040 (         GETKEY NAMES*DAT LASTNAME 10 20) 1040 SB
225 2002 1685 (         would cause GETKEY to read the data base file NAMES.DAT and extract the) 1685 SB
225 2061 1777 (         "LASTNAME" field from each record, starting at position 10 and ending at charac-) 1777 SB
225 2120 1823 (         ter 20.  GETKEY builds a table in memory consisting of each particular LASTNAME) 1823 SB
225 2180 1717 (         field, along with its 16-bit record number location within the file.  The GETKEY) 1717 SB
225 2239 1804 (         program then sorts this list and writes a new file, called LASTNAME.KEY. This list,) 1804 SB
225 2298 1796 (         sometimes called an inverted index, is an alphabetical list of LASTNAME fields with) 1796 SB
225 2357 840 (         their corresponding record numbers.) 840 SB
225 2475 1740 (          You could rename the program shown above to QUERY, and modify it so that it) 1740 SB
225 2535 1547 (         reads a sorted key file into memory.  The command line might appear as) 1547 SB
225 2653 989 (         QUERY NAMES.DAT LASTNAME.KEY) 989 SB
1154 2830 92 (4-19) 92 SB
