0 0 0 fC
225 227 1627 (   Instead of reading a number, the QUERY program reads an alphanumeric string) 1627 SB
225 286 1718 (   which is a particular key to find in the NAMES.DAT data base.  Because the LAST-) 1718 SB
225 345 1658 (   NAME.KEY list is sorted, you can find a particular entry quickly by performing a) 1658 SB
225 405 1698 (   binary search, similar to looking up a name in the telephone directory.  Start at both) 1698 SB
225 464 1674 (   ends of the list and examine the entry halfway in between and, if not matched, split) 1674 SB
225 523 1724 (   either the upper half or the lower half for the next search.  You will quickly reach the) 1724 SB
225 582 1633 (   item you are looking for, in log2\(n\) steps, where you will find the corresponding) 1633 SB
225 641 1753 (   record number.  Fetch and display this record at the console as the program illustrates.) 1753 SB
225 760 1689 (      At this point, you are just getting started.  With a little more work, you can allow) 1689 SB
225 819 1727 (   a fixed grouping size, which differs from the 128-byte record shown above.  You can) 1727 SB
225 878 1616 (   accomplish this by keeping track of the record number as well as the byte offset) 1616 SB
225 937 1627 (   within the record.  Knowing the group size, you can randomly access the record) 1627 SB
225 996 1690 (   containing the proper group, offset to the beginning of the group within the record,) 1690 SB
225 1055 1256 (   and read sequentially until the group size has been exhausted.) 1256 SB
225 1174 1675 (      Finally, you can improve QUERY considerably by allowing Boolean expressions) 1675 SB
225 1233 1657 (   that compute the set of records that satisfy several relationships, such as a LAST-) 1657 SB
225 1292 1669 (   NAME between HARDY and LAUREL and an AGE less than 45.  Display all the) 1669 SB
225 1351 1642 (   records that fit this description.  Finally, if your lists are getting too big to fit into) 1642 SB
225 1410 1270 (   memory, randomly access your key files from the disk as well.) 1270 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 1529 829 (   4.4 Construction of an RSX Program) 829 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1647 1681 (      This section describes the standard prefix of a Resident System Extension \(RSX\)) 1681 SB
225 1706 1703 (   and illustrates the construction of an RSX with an example. \(See Section 1.6.4 for a) 1703 SB
225 1765 1702 (   discussion of how RSXs operate under CP/M 3.\) RSX programs are usually written) 1702 SB
225 1825 1685 (   in assembler, but you can use other languages if the interface between the language) 1685 SB
225 1884 1250 (   and the calling conventions of the BDOS are set up properly.) 1250 SB
