0 0 0 fC
225 227 1717 (      The prev: field contains the address of the preceding RSX in memory or location 5) 1717 SB
225 286 835 (    if the RSX is the first RSX in the chain.) 835 SB
225 405 1705 (      The remove: field controls whether the RSX is removed from memory by the next) 1705 SB
225 464 1755 (    call to the LOADER module via BDOS function 59.  If the remove: flag is OFFH, the) 1755 SB
225 523 1629 (    LOADER removes the RSX from memory.  Note that the CCP always calls the) 1629 SB
225 582 1704 (    LOADER module during a warm start operation.  An RSX that remains in memory) 1704 SB
225 641 1693 (    past warm start because its remove: flag is zero, must set the flag at its termination) 1693 SB
225 700 1314 (    to ensure its removal from memory at the following warm start.) 1314 SB
225 819 1630 (      The nonbank: field controls when the RSX is loaded.  If the field is OFFH, the) 1630 SB
225 878 1609 (    LOADER only loads the module into memory on nonbanked CP/M 3 systems.) 1609 SB
225 937 1695 (    Otherwise, the RSX is loaded into memory under both banked and nonbanked ver-) 1695 SB
225 996 389 (    sions of CP/M 3.) 389 SB
225 1115 1702 (      The loader: flag identifies the LOADER RSX.  When the LOADER module loads) 1702 SB
225 1174 1758 (    an RSX into memory, it sets this prefix flag of the loaded RSX to zero.  However, the) 1758 SB
225 1233 1752 (    loader: flag in the LOADER's prefix contains OFFH.  Thus, this flag identifies the last) 1752 SB
225 1292 1025 (    RSX in the chain, which is always the LOADER.) 1025 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c34a font
225 1410 609 (    4.4.2 Example of RSX Use) 609 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c33d font
225 1529 1586 (      These two sample programs illustrate the use of an RSX program.  The first) 1586 SB
225 1588 1648 (    program, CALLVERS, prints a message to the console and then makes a BDOS) 1648 SB
225 1647 1657 (    Function 12 call to obtain the CP/M 3 version number.  CALLVERS repeats this) 1657 SB
225 1706 1779 (    sequence five times before terminating.  The second program, ECHOVERS, is an RSX) 1779 SB
225 1765 1658 (    that intercepts the BDOS Function 12 call made by CALLVERS, prints a second) 1658 SB
225 1825 1699 (    message, and returns the version 0031H to CALLVERS.  Although this example is) 1699 SB
225 1884 1755 (    simple, it illustrates BDOS function interception, stack swapping, and BDOS function) 1755 SB
225 1943 457 (    calls within an RSX.) 457 SB
1154 2830 92 (4-22) 92 SB
225 92 742 (4.4 Construction of an RSX Program) 742 SB
1597 92 578 (CP/M 3 Programmer's Guide) 578 SB
EJ RS
